WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:05.430
"The Batman" is finally here, but what's up
with those twin bouncers?

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.030
And what's going on with Selina and her friend
from the photo?

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.210
Who's really having the last laugh?

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:15.239
Here's the truth about some lesser-known characters
in "The Batman."

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:20.720
It's hard to watch "The Batman" and not realize
that John Turturro's Carmine Falcone is a

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.570
major player in Gotham.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:27.860
But at the same time, his presence is so shadowy
and his backstory so complex that you might

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:32.149
not realize how thoroughly he has influenced
this city in decline.

00:00:32.149 --> 00:00:37.079
The Falcone character has been in orbit around
Batman for some time, even if he wasn't introduced

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:42.420
until the late '80s, long after characters
like Joker and the Penguin had been established.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:47.949
Tom Wilkinson made for a menacing Falcone
in "Batman Begins," and John Doman fleshed

00:00:47.949 --> 00:00:51.660
out the mobster stereotype quite a bit on
the TV series "Gotham."

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.850
But Turturro's Falcone is a different beast.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:59.010
From the first scenes of "The Batman," you
can see the newspaper clippings on the wall

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.460
celebrating the arrest of Salvatore Maroni.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:06.590
Maroni was once Falcone's chief rival, and
his arrest opened the city for Falcone's ascension

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.790
up the ladder of organized crime.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:15.080
The crime lord has his fingers in many pies,
but also owns the Iceberg Lounge and its "club

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.300
within the club," the mob hangout 44 Below.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.880
This is where Selina Kyle's mother Maria once
worked, and Selina has been a regular face

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.130
there since she was a child, and is now employed
there.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:30.300
As Carmine later confesses in a moment of
weakness, he killed Maria — and if he'd

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.180
had his way, he would have killed Selina too.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:34.770
Then there's the Wayne family.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.590
Carmine was once shot in the chest and showed
up on the doorstep to Wayne Manor, where Thomas

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:39.750
Wayne saved his life.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:44.570
A young Bruce, hiding in the shadows of a
stairwell, witnessed the entire incident.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:49.540
It is moments like this that made Bruce grow
up thinking his father was a great, noble

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:53.050
man whose senseless death needs to be avenged.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:58.030
As Carmine is all too willing to tell an adult
Bruce Wayne, however, Thomas got his hands

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:00.990
dirty too, leading to the death of a newspaper
reporter.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:07.070
The front door at the Iceberg Lounge is manned
by two intimidating (if somewhat dim) henchmen

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:09.880
serving the Penguin and ultimately Falcone.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:14.120
"The twins," as they are called throughout
the film, are played by Max and Charlie Carver

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:15.800
from MTV's "Teen Wolf."

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.440
In an early scene of "The Batman," the Dark
Knight shows up at their door and says:

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:20.720
"Know who I am?"

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.840
And when they don't let him in, he uses his
fists to remind them.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.080
In a later scene echoing the moment, Bruce
Wayne shows up and asks the same question.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:32.340
They allow him in, oblivious to the symmetry.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:36.700
On one hand, they might just be low-level
bouncers with the gimmicky bonus of being

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:42.440
twin brothers; on the other, perhaps they
could be Gotham supervillains in the making

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:46.819
In the comics, "Tweedledee and Tweedledum"
are two gangsters/supervillains whose battles

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:52.590
against Batman date all the way back to 1943,
much longer than Falcone.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:54.959
One is a good fighter, the other a good thinker.

00:02:54.959 --> 00:03:00.000
They are actually cousins who just so happen
to look alike, and so Deever Tweed and his

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.580
cousin Dumfrey lean into their not-so-complimentary
nicknames, as well as embracing their similar

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.550
appearances to confuse enemies.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:12.650
In some incarnations over the years, Tweedledee
and Tweedledum have been criminal masterminds,

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:16.970
content to plot out elaborate crimes and let
their henchmen handle the execution.

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:22.990
In other tales, they themselves are henchmen
of someone like the Joker or the Mad Hatter.

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:27.950
So could these twins be henchmen for the Penguin,
who seems to be making his own power play

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:29.440
in Gotham's underworld?

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.989
Could future films have Robert Pattinson's
Batman squaring off against Tweedledee and

00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:33.989
Tweedledum?

00:03:33.989 --> 00:03:36.950
Such thoughts make us curiouser and curiouser.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:43.910
Determined to change Gotham through more straightforward
means, Bella Reál feels a lot like Aaron

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:46.270
Eckhart's Harvey Dent in "The Dark Knight."

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:51.530
She's running for office, she's eager for
a new day, and she's determined to win.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:55.319
Also like Dent, she's oblivious to the daytime
activities of the Batman.

00:03:55.319 --> 00:03:58.090
"You really could be doing more for this city.

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:01.630
Your family has a history of philanthropy,
but as far as I can tell, you're not doing

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:02.630
anything."

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:07.410
Robert Pattinson's Batman doesn't seem too
eager to play politics, but that changes when

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:10.600
the two come crashing together in the film's
final act.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:15.000
As water floods into the arena where they're
situated, Reál is urged to flee.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.150
But she chooses instead to try and help, and
is nearly assassinated in the process.

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:24.800
This much seems clear: She's won a newfound
respect from Bruce Wayne that will likely

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:26.710
carry forward into a sequel.

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:29.090
So where does the character go from here?

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:33.720
Perhaps as a political ally to Bruce, perhaps
as a transformative figure in Gotham.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:37.940
We can only hope that, unlike Dent, she stays
on the right side of the law.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:42.880
Although she's barely glimpsed, the presence
of Selina Kyle's friend is felt throughout

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:44.300
much of the film.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:48.620
She's the "other woman" in the incriminating
photo with the recently murdered Mayor Mitchell.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:53.470
It is her visage that sics Batman, like a
watchdog, on the scent of the Penguin.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:58.250
When Selina Kyle overhears Anika's name mentioned,
she's the one who hurries to tell her friend

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:03.490
that the heat is on — in the process revealing
herself to Batman as the Catwoman.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:05.780
But she may be more than just a friend.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:10.180
In an interview shortly before the film's
release, Zoë Kravitz claimed that her Selina

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:14.400
Kyle is bisexual in "The Batman" and has an
intimate relationship with Anika.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.260
She told Pedestrian,
"That's definitely the way I interpreted that.

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:21.460
They had some kind of romantic relationship."

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:25.560
Director Matt Reeves, however, seems to respectfully
disagree.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:29.240
He told the same outlet,
"I don't think we meant to go directly in

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:32.490
that way, but you can interpret it that way
for sure.

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:37.290
She has an intimacy with that character and
it's a tremendous and deep caring for that

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:42.550
character, more so than a sexual thing, but
there was meant to be quite an intimate relationship

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:43.570
between them."

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:47.860
Regardless, the story machinations continue
when Batman catches Selina in an attempt to

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:51.750
steal back Anika's passport, in a desperate
attempt to help her leave the country.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:55.500
But the writing is on the wall, and Anika
eventually turns up dead.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.900
So is Anika anything more than a mere plot
device?

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:02.430
Like many characters in "The Batman," her
name doesn't seem to appear in any Batman

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:03.810
stories, ever.

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:08.050
Almost certainly, she's a new character created
by Reeves and co-writer Peter Craig.

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:12.320
As such, Anika means more than you might realize
for a few additional reasons.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:18.020
First, she symbolizes Reeves and Craig's preference
for creating a credible detective tale rather

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:21.520
than a fan-servicing movie filled with Google-able
names.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:26.711
Secondly, she represents a fate that seems
all too common in Gotham: used, spent, then

00:06:26.711 --> 00:06:30.380
tossed in a trash can by men of wealth and
influence.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:34.729
Anika got caught in the cogs of Gotham's elite,
and was mangled in the process.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:39.790
Supposedly, Barry Keoghan was cast in "The
Batman" as officer Stanley Merkel.

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:44.229
In the comics, Merkel has a fairly short career
as James Gordon's partner before eventually

00:06:44.229 --> 00:06:46.740
meeting his demise at the hands of The Hangman.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:50.830
As for Keoghan's actual role in the movie,
there's some pretty strong indications that

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:54.820
the casting news we all heard was a lie — or
a joke.

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:58.729
Although it's not confirmed in the film itself,
it's pretty clear that Keoghan's cackling

00:06:58.729 --> 00:07:01.600
Arkham neighbor to the Riddler is the Joker.

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:06.050
If "The Batman" lands a sequel, there's a
strong possibility we'll be seeing more of

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:07.290
Keoghan's character…

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:08.810
whatever his name may be.

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:10.240
"It seems too easy."

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:12.210
"It's never easy with the Joker."

